# CSS: utilities, no redundant classes, no overrides

## Prefer styling the specific element

Put styles on the element that needs them. Do not set a broad rule and then override it on children or wrappers.

```css
/* ❌ BAD — bold on all links, then undo on cards */
a { font-weight: 700; }
.project { font-weight: 400; }

/* ✅ GOOD — bold only where links should look bold */
.prose a { font-weight: 700; }
```

If inheritance forces an override, fix the source rule instead of stacking resets.

## No redundant classes

Do not create a class that only restates defaults or packs one-off properties that belong elsewhere.

- Do not set `font-weight: 400` / `normal` unless you are correcting a real inheritance problem (prefer fixing the source — see above).
- Do not shrink or restyle type “just because” on a subtitle/meta line unless asked.
- Do not invent a component class (e.g. `.project-subtitle`) for a single shared concern like muted color.

## Prefer utility classes for shared concerns

If the only meaningful style is something reusable (e.g. secondary/muted text), add or reuse a utility and apply it in the markup.

```html
<!-- ❌ BAD -->
<div class="project-subtitle">…</div>
/* .project-subtitle { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: 0.95em; font-weight: 400; } */

<!-- ✅ GOOD -->
<div class="secondary">…</div>
/* .secondary { color: var(--text-secondary); } */
```

Keep utilities single-purpose. Layout wrappers (e.g. `.project-text` for flex + gap) are fine when they own real structure, not decorative restatements.
